\documentclass[article]{jss}

\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs}

%% -- Article metainformation --------------------------------------------

\author{Hassan Doosti\\Macquarie University}
\Plainauthor{Hassan Doosti}

\title{\pkg{tiltdens}: Tilted and Data-Sharpened Density Estimation in \proglang{R}}
\Plaintitle{tiltdens: Tilted and Data-Sharpened Density Estimation in R}
\Shorttitle{\pkg{tiltdens}: Tilted Density Estimation in \proglang{R}}

\Abstract{
  A kernel density estimator built from a non-negative kernel cannot converge
  faster than $O_p(n^{-4/5})$, however smooth the underlying density may be.
  Infinite-order kernels break that barrier, but the estimates they produce take
  negative values and oscillate in the tails: they are not densities, and they
  suggest structure that is not present. \pkg{tiltdens} implements a resolution
  of this tension, in which a conventional kernel estimator is perturbed toward
  an infinite-order one, either by re-weighting the observations or by moving
  them. The result inherits the faster rate while remaining a proper,
  non-negative density.

  The package implements the two criteria of \cite{doosti+hall:2016} and
  \cite{doosti+hall+mateu:2018}. We show that both reduce to the same convex
  quadratic program over the probability simplex, differing only in a single
  linear term, so that one solver serves both and the solution is unique. That
  reformulation is what makes the methods practical, and it extends without
  modification to blocked weights, shape constraints and multivariate data. No
  implementation of tilted density estimation was previously available in any
  language.
}

\Keywords{tilting, data sharpening, density estimation, quadratic programming,
  infinite-order kernels, \proglang{R}}
\Plainkeywords{tilting, data sharpening, density estimation, quadratic
  programming, infinite-order kernels, R}

\Address{
  Hassan Doosti\\
  School of Mathematical and Physical Sciences\\
  Macquarie University\\
  Sydney NSW 2109, Australia\\
  E-mail: \email{hassan.doosti@mq.edu.au}
}

\begin{document}

<<setup, echo=FALSE, results=hide>>=
options(prompt = "R> ", continue = "+  ", width = 76, useFancyQuotes = FALSE)
library("tiltdens")
set.seed(2016)
@

%% ======================================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Let $X_1,\dots,X_n$ be a random sample from a density $f$, and let
\begin{equation}\label{eq:conventional}
  \tilde f(x \mid h) = \frac{1}{nh}\sum_{i=1}^{n} K\!\left(\frac{x - X_i}{h}\right)
\end{equation}
be the conventional kernel estimator. When $K$ is a bounded, symmetric
probability density and $f$ has two bounded derivatives, $\tilde f$ converges at
rate $O_p(n^{-4/5})$ in integrated squared error, and this cannot be improved
however smooth $f$ is \citep{stone:1980}. Faster rates require a kernel of
higher order, which necessarily takes negative values on part of its support.

The price is well known. A higher-order estimate is not a density: it dips below
zero, most visibly in the tails, and it oscillates there in a way that invites
the reader to see modes that are not present. Practitioners consequently tend to
prefer conventional kernels even when the rate penalty is understood, and
\cite{doosti+hall:2016} open by observing exactly this.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Perturbed estimators}

The resolution proposed by \cite{doosti+hall:2016} is to keep the conventional
kernel and perturb the estimator toward a higher-order one. Two perturbations
are available. \emph{Tilting} re-weights the observations,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:tilt}
  \hat f(x \mid h, p) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} p_i \, K_h(x - X_i),
  \qquad p_i \ge 0, \quad \sum_i p_i = 1,
\end{equation}
where $K_h(u) = h^{-1}K(u/h)$, and \emph{data sharpening} moves them,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sharpen}
  \hat f(x \mid h, q) = \frac{1}{nh}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
    K\!\left(\frac{x - X_i - q_i}{h}\right).
\end{equation}
Because $K$ is a proper density and $p$ is a probability vector, the estimator
at~(\ref{eq:tilt}) is non-negative and integrates to one by construction; the
same holds for~(\ref{eq:sharpen}). The perturbation is chosen so that the result
sits as close as possible to a comparator $\check f$ built from an
infinite-order kernel, which transfers the comparator's convergence rate without
its defects. Tilting in this sense goes back to \cite{grenander:1956}, and was
developed for constrained function estimation by \cite{hall+presnell:1999} and
\cite{hall+huang:2002}.

The 2016 construction is accurate but expensive, since it requires the
comparator to be computed and the distance to it minimised.
\cite{doosti+hall+mateu:2018} remove the comparator entirely, choosing $h$ and
$p$ together by minimising a cross-validation criterion. That is the method we
expect most users to want, and it is the package's recommended default.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Existing software}\label{sec:existing}

Kernel density estimation is well served in \proglang{R}. \pkg{ks}
\citep{duong:2007} and \pkg{KernSmooth} provide conventional and higher-order
estimators with a range of bandwidth selectors; \pkg{sm} and \pkg{kedd} cover
similar ground. None of them offers any perturbation machinery, so none can
deliver a higher-order rate with a guaranteed non-negative estimate.

Shape-constrained estimation is also available, but by a different route.
\pkg{logcondens} \citep{duembgen+rufibach:2011} maximises a likelihood under
log-concavity, which imposes shape by restricting the estimator class rather
than by re-weighting observations.

The package closest in name is \pkg{sharpData}, which implements data sharpening
following \cite{choi+hall+rousson:2000}. That work concerns \emph{local linear
regression}, not density estimation, and the two problems share only the
sharpening idea.

To our knowledge no package, in \proglang{R} or in any other language,
implements tilted density estimation.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Contribution}\label{sec:contribution}

We separate two things. The first is an implementation of two published methods
that previously had none. The second, and the reason a software paper is
warranted rather than a code release, is what had to be added to make those
methods usable. In order of importance:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{One convex program.} Both selection criteria reduce to the same
  quadratic program over the probability simplex, differing only in one linear
  term, and the quadratic form is positive semidefinite for any kernel
  (Section~\ref{sec:convex}). The reformulation gives a unique solution from
  a standard solver, in place of a search over a high-dimensional simplex.
\item \emph{Optimised block boundaries} (Section~\ref{sec:blocks}). The 2016
  paper treats the boundaries between blocks of equal weight as part of the
  optimisation but does not say how; reading block sums off a two-dimensional
  cumulative sum makes exhaustive search affordable. A second placement rule,
  at the troughs of a pilot estimate, follows a suggestion in the 2018 paper.
\item \emph{Shape constraints} (Section~\ref{sec:constraints}), following
  \cite{hall+huang:2002}, and their combination with the cross-validation
  criterion, which the 2018 paper suggests but does not develop.
\item \emph{Multivariate data} (Section~\ref{sec:multivariate}). Described in
  Section~3.3 of the 2016 paper; no code existed.
\item \emph{A choice of kernel, with comparable bandwidths}
  (Section~\ref{sec:kernels}). The papers use the Gaussian throughout. The
  package offers the Laplace-convolution family for which the 2016 theory is
  stated, and the usual compact kernels, with bandwidths rescaled by the
  canonical factor of \cite{marron+nolan:1988} so that the choice of kernel
  changes the shape of the estimate rather than its degree of smoothing.
\item \emph{Numerical details that the papers leave implicit}
  (Section~\ref{sec:numerics}): quadrature for the oscillatory cross terms that
  adapts to the frequency, and a bounded search for the comparator bandwidth
  that avoids the spurious minima of an infinite-order cross-validation
  criterion.
\end{enumerate}

Sections~\ref{sec:usage} and~\ref{sec:illustration} demonstrate the package,
and Section~\ref{sec:comparison} places it among existing alternatives.

%% ======================================================================
\section{Perturbed density estimation}\label{sec:methods}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Comparator estimators}

Both criteria refer to an estimator built from a kernel of unlimited order. The
package provides two. The \emph{sinc} estimator uses
$L(u) = \sin(u)/(\pi u)$, whose Fourier transform is the indicator of $[-1,1]$;
the \emph{trapezoidal} or flat-top estimator of \cite{politis:2003} and
\cite{politis+romano:1999} uses
$L(u) = \{\cos(u) - \cos(2u)\}/(\pi u^2)$, whose transform is $1$ on $[-1,1]$
and tapers linearly to zero at $|z| = 2$. Both converge quickly when $f$ is
smooth, and both misbehave in the manner described above.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{The distance criterion}\label{sec:crit2016}

\cite{doosti+hall:2016} choose $\theta = (h, p)$ to minimise
$\|\hat f(\cdot \mid \theta) - \check f\|$ in $L_2$. Their Theorem~1 establishes
that the minimiser inherits the convergence rate of $\check f$, and their
Theorem~2 that it does so uniformly over classes of densities. The estimator is
therefore rate-optimal in the sense of \cite{stone:1980} while remaining a
proper density.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{The cross-validation criterion}\label{sec:crit2018}

\cite{doosti+hall+mateu:2018} replace the comparator with
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cv}
  \mathrm{CV}(h, p) = \int \hat f(x \mid h,p)^2\,dx
    - \frac{2}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} \hat f_{-i}(X_i \mid h,p),
\end{equation}
where $\hat f_{-i}$ omits the term in $X_i$. As the authors note, $p$ need not be
re-standardised after the deletion, which saves computation. Minimising
(\ref{eq:cv}) over $h$ and $p$ jointly attains an integrated squared error of
strictly smaller order than the $n^{-4/5}$ available to $\tilde f$.

%% ======================================================================
\section{One convex program}\label{sec:convex}

Write $A$ for the $n \times n$ matrix with entries
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gram}
  A_{ij} = \int K_h(x - X_i)\,K_h(x - X_j)\,dx = (K_h \ast K_h)(X_i - X_j),
\end{equation}
so that $\int \hat f(x \mid h,p)^2 dx = p^\top A p$ exactly. Both criteria then
have the form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:qp}
  p^\top A p - \frac{2}{n}\, p^\top b + \mathrm{const},
\end{equation}
and \emph{they differ only in $b$}. For the criterion of
Section~\ref{sec:crit2016},
$b_i = \sum_j \int K_h(x - X_i) L_g(x - X_j)\,dx$, the cross terms against the
comparator, and the constant is $\|\check f\|^2$. For the criterion of
Section~\ref{sec:crit2018}, $b_j = \sum_{i \ne j} K_h(X_i - X_j)$, obtained from
the leave-one-out fit, and the constant is zero.

Minimising (\ref{eq:qp}) subject to $p \ge 0$ and $\sum_i p_i = 1$ is a
quadratic program over the probability simplex. It is convex: the Fourier
transform of $K \ast K$ is $\phi_K^2 \ge 0$, and a function with a non-negative
Fourier transform has a positive semidefinite Gram matrix. Equivalently,
$p^\top A p = \int \hat f^2 \ge 0$ for every $p$. \emph{This holds whatever
kernel is used}, which is what allows the package to offer a choice of $K$
without further argument.

The practical consequence is that the solution is unique and is found by a
standard method: \pkg{tiltdens} uses the dual algorithm of
\cite{goldfarb+idnani:1983} through \pkg{quadprog} \citep{quadprog}, falling back
to an accelerated projected-gradient scheme when the quadratic form is too
ill-conditioned. For $m \le 6$ variables an exact enumeration of the possible
supports is faster still, and is used inside the search of
Section~\ref{sec:blocks}.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Blocked weights and their boundaries}\label{sec:blocks}

Section~4.1 of \cite{doosti+hall:2016} restricts the weights to $m$ distinct
values over contiguous blocks of the order statistics, and Algorithm~A of
\cite{doosti+hall+mateu:2018} does the same with approximately equal blocks.
Writing $p = G q$ for the $n \times m$ block indicator $G$, (\ref{eq:qp}) becomes
an $m$-dimensional program in $q$ with $G^\top A G$ and $G^\top b$ in place of
$A$ and $b$.

The 2016 paper treats the block \emph{boundaries} as part of the optimisation.
Doing so naively is expensive, but the block sums of $A$ can be read off a
two-dimensional cumulative sum in constant time, and the resulting program has
only $m$ variables, so every admissible set of boundaries can be examined for
$m \le 3$. Above that the package refines the boundaries by coordinate descent
from an equally spaced start.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Shape constraints}\label{sec:constraints}

Since $\hat f$ is linear in $p$, a shape requirement evaluated on a grid
$t_1 < \dots < t_M$ is a set of linear inequalities. With
$\Phi_{kj} = K_h(t_k - X_j)$ the fitted curve is $\Phi p$, so monotonicity is
$(\Phi_{k+1,\cdot} - \Phi_{k,\cdot})\,p \ge 0$, and unimodality with the mode at
$t_{k_0}$ is that condition up to $k_0$ and its reverse after. The program stays
convex; only its feasible set gains rows. The mode location is unknown, so the
program is solved for each candidate mode and the best solution retained, the
device used by \cite{hall+huang:2002}.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Multivariate data}\label{sec:multivariate}

Section~3.3 of \cite{doosti+hall:2016} replaces $K$ with a product kernel
$K_{\mathrm{pr}}(x) = \prod_{j} K(x_j)$ and takes a scalar bandwidth. Every
quantity the method needs then factorises,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:factorise}
  \int K_{\mathrm{pr},h}(x - u)\,L_{\mathrm{pr},g}(x - v)\,dx
   = \prod_{j=1}^{l} \int K_h(x_j - u_j)\,L_g(x_j - v_j)\,dx_j,
\end{equation}
so the $l$-variate matrices are elementwise products of the univariate ones and
the program is unchanged. The weights remain an $n$-vector on the simplex.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Kernels}\label{sec:kernels}

Nothing above requires $K$ to be Gaussian. The Gram matrix (\ref{eq:gram}) needs
$K \ast K$, the cross terms need the Fourier transform $\phi_K$, and evaluation
needs $K$ itself; given those three, the program is unchanged, and it is convex
for every $K$ by the argument following (\ref{eq:qp}). The package provides the
Gaussian; the $k$-fold convolutions of a Laplace density for $k = 1, \dots, 4$,
whose transform is $(1 + t^2)^{-k}$ and for which the smoothness condition of
Section~3.1 of \cite{doosti+hall:2016} is stated; and the Epanechnikov,
biweight, triweight and triangular kernels. For the Laplace family
$K \ast K$ is the $2k$-fold convolution and is available in closed form; for
the compact kernels it is computed once by quadrature, piecewise between the
points where it is not smooth, and interpolated.

A bandwidth is only meaningful relative to the kernel it scales. The package
therefore rescales its default bandwidth by the canonical factor
$\delta_K = \{R(K)/\mu_2(K)^2\}^{1/5}$ of \cite{marron+nolan:1988}, where
$R(K) = \int K^2$ is read off $K \ast K$ at zero and $\mu_2(K)$ is the second
moment. With that rescaling the same numerical bandwidth smooths by the same
amount whichever kernel is used; in the example of Section~\ref{sec:usage} the
spread in integrated squared error across four kernels falls from about
$2$ to $1.1$.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Numerical details}\label{sec:numerics}

Two computations deserve care, because both are easy to get subtly wrong.

\emph{The cross terms.} Every inner product between two kernel estimators
reduces, by Parseval's identity, to an integral of the form
$\int_0^{c} w(z)\cos(tz)\,dz$ with $t = (X_i - X_j)/h$, and there are $n^2$ of
them. The integrand oscillates $t/2\pi$ times over the range, so a fixed
quadrature rule fails once $|t|$ is large -- that is, once the pairwise distance
between observations is large relative to the bandwidth, which is the usual
case in the tails. A fixed rule with a dozen or so nodes, for instance, is
accurate to $10^{-6}$ at $|t| = 5$, to $4\%$ at $|t| = 20$, and is
meaningless beyond $|t| = 40$. The package scales the number of Gauss--Legendre
panels with $\max|t|$, evaluates all $n^2$ integrals as one matrix product
against the shared nodes, and is checked against the closed form
$\int_0^1 \cos(tz)\,dz = \sin(t)/t$ at $|t| = 60$.

\emph{The comparator bandwidth.} Least-squares cross-validation for an
infinite-order kernel is multimodal in $h$, and its deepest minimum is
frequently a spurious one at a very small bandwidth: beyond the frequency at
which the empirical characteristic function decays into sampling noise, the
criterion is fitting that noise. The package searches a frequency grid
$q = 1/h$ and caps it at twice the frequency identified by the flat-top rule of
\cite{politis:2003}, taking the minimum within that range. The cap is a
judgement, documented and adjustable through an argument, and the criterion
over the whole grid is returned so that the choice can be inspected.

%% ======================================================================
\section{Using the package}\label{sec:usage}

A bimodal sample serves throughout.

<<data>>=
x <- c(rnorm(50, -1.5), rnorm(50, 1.5))
@

The recommended entry point is \code{tilt_density_cv}, which implements the
criterion of Section~\ref{sec:crit2018}.

<<fit-cv>>=
fit <- tilt_density_cv(x)
fit
@

Fitted objects inherit from class \code{"density"}, so \code{plot},
\code{lines} and \code{points} behave as they do for \code{stats::density}.
The point of the exercise is visible in two numbers.

<<positivity>>=
c(sinc = min(sinc_density(x)$y), tilted = min(fit$y))
@

The comparator takes negative values; the tilted estimate cannot.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{The distance criterion and block structure}

\code{tilt_density} implements Section~\ref{sec:crit2016}. The argument \code{m}
sets the number of distinct weights and \code{breaks} how their boundaries are
placed: \code{"equal"} is Algorithm~A, \code{"modal"} places boundaries at the
troughs of a pilot estimate, and \code{"optimal"} searches them as in
Section~\ref{sec:blocks}.

<<breaks>>=
sapply(c("equal", "modal", "optimal"), function(b)
  tilt_density(x, m = 3, breaks = b)$distance2)
@

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Kernels and canonical bandwidths}

The kernels of Section~\ref{sec:kernels} are selected by name.

<<kernels>>=
tilt_kernels()
@

<<canonical>>=
round(sapply(c("gaussian", "epanechnikov", "biweight"),
             bw_canonical_factor), 4)
@

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Shape constraints and multivariate data}

<<constraint, eval=FALSE>>=
tilt_density(x, m = Inf, constraint = "unimodal")
@

<<multivariate, eval=FALSE>>=
X <- cbind(x, c(rnorm(50, -1), rnorm(50, 1)))
plot(tilt_density(X))
@

%% ======================================================================
\section{Illustrations}\label{sec:illustration}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Simulation}\label{sec:simulation}

A brief simulation, in the design of both papers, shows where the perturbed
estimators help and where they do not. Four of the eight normal-mixture
densities of \cite{marron+wand:1992} are used, chosen to span the range from
simple to structured: the Gaussian, the kurtotic unimodal (a spike on a broad
base), the separated bimodal, and the skewed bimodal. Samples are of size
$n = 100$. The estimators compared are the conventional Gaussian kernel
estimator with a robust normal-reference bandwidth; the sinc and trapezoidal
estimators with a cross-validated bandwidth; the tilted estimator of
Section~\ref{sec:crit2016} with one weight per observation ($T_n$) and with
three distinct weights and optimised boundaries ($T_3$); and the
cross-validation estimator of Section~\ref{sec:crit2018} with up to three
distinct weights. The replication script reproduces
Table~\ref{tab:mise} in about ten minutes.

<<sim-table, echo=FALSE, results=tex>>=
files <- list.files("sim", pattern = "^ise_.*\\.rds$", full.names = TRUE)
order_names <- c("Gau","SkU","StS","KtU","Out","Bim","SeB","SkB")
labels <- c(Gau = "Gaussian", SkU = "Skewed unimodal", StS = "Strongly skewed",
            KtU = "Kurtotic unimodal", Out = "Outlier", Bim = "Bimodal",
            SeB = "Separated bimodal", SkB = "Skewed bimodal")
res <- lapply(files, readRDS); names(res) <- sub("^ise_(.*)\\.rds$", "\\1", basename(files))
res <- res[intersect(order_names, names(res))]
n_rep <- if (length(res)) nrow(res[[1]]) else NA
cat("\\begin{table}[t!]\n\\centering \n")
cat("\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n\\toprule \n")
cat("Density & Conv. & Sinc & Trap. & $T_n$ & $T_3$ & CV \\\\\n\\midrule \n")
for (nm in names(res)) {
  m <- colMeans(res[[nm]], na.rm = TRUE) * 1000
  best <- which.min(m)
  cells <- sprintf("%.2f", m)
  cells[best] <- paste0("\\textbf{", cells[best], "}")
  cat(labels[nm], " & ", paste(cells, collapse = " & "), " \\\\\n", sep = "")
}
cat("\\bottomrule \n\\end{tabular}\n")
cat(sprintf("\\caption{Mean integrated squared error, multiplied by 1000, for $n = 100$ over %s replicates. The smallest value in each row is in bold. Conv.: conventional Gaussian kernel estimator; Trap.: trapezoidal estimator; $T_n$ and $T_3$: tilted estimators with $n$ and 3 distinct weights; CV: the cross-validation estimator.}\n", n_rep))
cat("\\label{tab:mise}\n\\end{table}\n")
@

Table~\ref{tab:mise} shows the pattern both papers report, and we do not wish
to oversell it. Where the density is simple and smooth, the conventional
estimator is hard to beat, and the perturbed estimators roughly match it. Where
the density has sharp structure -- a spike at the mode, well-separated modes --
the perturbed estimators are clearly better, because they can follow the
comparator into that structure without following it below zero. The tilted
estimators never do worse than the comparator they are built from, which is the
content of Theorem~1 of \cite{doosti+hall:2016} expressed numerically.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Old Faithful}\label{sec:oldfaithful}

Both papers analyse eruption durations of the Old Faithful geyser, using the
107 observations discussed by \cite{silverman:1986}. We use the 272 durations
in \proglang{R}'s \code{faithful} data set, which come from a later collection
period; the substantive question -- is the density bimodal? -- is the same.

<<faithful-fit>>=
x <- faithful$eruptions
fit_cv   <- tilt_density_cv(x)
fit_sinc <- sinc_density(x, bw = fit_cv$bw, from = 1, to = 6)
c(sinc = min(fit_sinc$y), tilted = min(fit_cv$y))
@

\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
<<faithful-plot, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE, width=7, height=3.8>>=
par(mar = c(4, 4, 1, 1))
conv <- density(x, bw = bw_nrd_robust(x), from = 1, to = 6)
plot(fit_cv$x, fit_cv$y, type = "l", lwd = 2, xlab = "Eruption duration (minutes)",
     ylab = "Density", ylim = range(0, fit_sinc$y, fit_cv$y, conv$y))
lines(fit_sinc, col = "red", lty = 2)
lines(conv, col = "grey40", lty = 3)
abline(h = 0, col = "grey70")
rug(x, col = "grey60")
legend("topleft", c("Tilted (CV)", "Sinc", "Conventional"),
       col = c("black", "red", "grey40"), lty = c(1, 2, 3), lwd = c(2, 1, 1), bty = "n")
@
\caption{Old Faithful eruption durations. The sinc estimator dips well below
zero between and beyond the modes; the tilted estimate tracks it where it is
sensible and cannot follow it below zero. The conventional estimator, with a
normal-reference bandwidth, is the most reluctant of the three to separate the
modes.}
\label{fig:faithful}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:faithful} shows the three estimates. The sinc estimator's
minimum of \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", min(fit_sinc$y))} is not a small defect: it is
comparable in magnitude to the density in the trough between the modes. The
tilted estimate has the same bandwidth and the same two modes, and its minimum
is \Sexpr{sprintf("%.1e", min(fit_cv$y))}.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Galaxy velocities}\label{sec:galaxies}

The 82 galaxy velocities of \cite{roeder:1990}, available as \code{galaxies} in
\pkg{MASS}, are the standard test case for multimodality: the astrophysical
question is whether the galaxies cluster, which would show as several modes.
Shape constraints give a direct way to ask it.

<<galaxies>>=
g <- MASS::galaxies / 1000
free     <- tilt_density(g, m = Inf)
unimodal <- tilt_density(g, m = Inf, constraint = "unimodal")
c(free = free$distance2, unimodal = unimodal$distance2)
@

Forcing the estimate to be unimodal moves it
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", unimodal$distance2 / free$distance2)} times further from
the infinite-order comparator. That ratio is an informal measure of how strongly
the data resist a single mode, in the spirit of the bandwidth test of
\cite{silverman:1981} but reading the answer off a distance rather than a
critical bandwidth.

\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
<<galaxies-plot, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE, width=7, height=3.8>>=
par(mar = c(4, 4, 1, 1))
plot(free$x, free$y, type = "l", lwd = 2, xlab = "Velocity (1000 km/s)",
     ylab = "Density", ylim = range(0, free$y, unimodal$y))
lines(unimodal$x, unimodal$y, col = "blue", lty = 2, lwd = 1.5)
abline(h = 0, col = "grey70")
rug(g, col = "grey60")
legend("topright", c("Tilted, unconstrained", "Tilted, unimodal"),
       col = c("black", "blue"), lty = c(1, 2), lwd = c(2, 1.5), bty = "n")
@
\caption{Galaxy velocities. The unconstrained tilted estimate shows the main
cluster and the outlying groups; the unimodal fit is what the data would have to
look like if there were one cluster, and it fits noticeably worse.}
\label{fig:galaxies}
\end{figure}

%% ======================================================================
\section{Comparison with existing packages}\label{sec:comparison}

Table~\ref{tab:packages} places \pkg{tiltdens} among the \proglang{R} packages a
user might reach for. The comparison is not that \pkg{tiltdens} is better than
\pkg{ks} at what \pkg{ks} does; it is that \pkg{tiltdens} does something none of
the others does. The combination in the last column -- an estimate that
converges at a higher-order rate \emph{and} is guaranteed to be a density -- is
not available elsewhere.

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Package & Higher-order rate & Non-negative & Shape constraints & Multivariate \\
\midrule
\pkg{ks}         & yes (higher-order kernels) & no  & no  & yes \\
\pkg{KernSmooth} & yes (higher-order kernels) & no  & no  & bivariate \\
\pkg{kdensity}   & no                          & yes & no  & no \\
\pkg{logcondens} & no                          & yes & log-concavity & no \\
\pkg{sharpData}  & regression only            & --  & --  & -- \\
\pkg{tiltdens}   & yes (by perturbation)      & yes & yes & yes \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Capabilities of \proglang{R} packages for density estimation.
\pkg{sharpData} implements data sharpening for local linear regression
\citep{choi+hall+rousson:2000}, not density estimation.}
\label{tab:packages}
\end{table}

Two of the alternatives deserve a word. \pkg{logcondens}
\citep{duembgen+rufibach:2011} also delivers a non-negative, shape-restricted
estimate, but by maximising a likelihood over log-concave densities; it is the
right tool when log-concavity is a credible assumption, and the wrong one when
the density is multimodal, as in Section~\ref{sec:galaxies}. \pkg{ks}
\citep{duong:2007} is the most complete general-purpose package and offers
higher-order kernels, but a user who wants their rate must accept an estimate
that goes negative; that is the trade-off \pkg{tiltdens} removes.

%% ======================================================================
\section{Summary and discussion}\label{sec:summary}

\pkg{tiltdens} implements the tilted and data-sharpened density estimators of
\cite{doosti+hall:2016} and the cross-validation criterion of
\cite{doosti+hall+mateu:2018}, which previously had no implementation in any
language. The estimators inherit the convergence rate of an infinite-order
kernel while remaining proper densities. The observation that makes them
practical is that both selection criteria reduce to a single convex quadratic
program over the probability simplex, differing only in one linear term: that
gives a unique solution, replaces a search over a high-dimensional simplex with
a standard solver, and extends without modification to blocked weights with
optimised boundaries, to shape constraints, and to multivariate data. A choice
of kernel is offered, with bandwidths rescaled so that the choice affects the
shape of the estimate rather than its degree of smoothing.

Several limitations should be stated plainly. Data sharpening is implemented
for univariate data only, and it is slow: the objective is not convex in the
shifts, so a stochastic search is used, and the papers' own finding is that
tilting is usually at least as accurate and far cheaper. Block structure rests
on the order statistics and has no natural multivariate counterpart, so
multivariate fits use one weight per observation. Shape constraints are imposed
on a grid, and a violation between grid points is possible in principle; in
practice it is of relative order $10^{-5}$ and the grid can be refined. The
cross-validation criterion for the comparator bandwidth is multimodal, and its
global minimum is often a spurious one at a very small bandwidth, so the search
is capped at a multiple of the frequency beyond which the empirical
characteristic function is indistinguishable from noise; that cap is a
judgement, documented and adjustable.

Two extensions are natural. \cite{doosti+hall+mateu:2018} report that tilted
estimators for spatially correlated data were under investigation, and the
machinery here -- a linear estimator whose weights lie on the simplex -- carries
over to that setting once the criterion is adapted. And the shape constraints of
Section~\ref{sec:constraints} are only those that are linear in the weights on
a grid; constraints on moments, on tail behaviour, or on the number of modes are
all expressible in the same form, and adding them is a matter of assembling the
inequality matrix.

